#!/usr/bin/env python3
# 12 October 2026: calibration of the differentiable sound bank on a CC0 corpus (task A3).
# Three steps, each one rerunnable:
#   prepare : decode every CC0 file of <calibrate.dir>/raw (Kenney packs, CC0 1.0), mono, 48 kHz, leading silence trimmed
#             to audio.preRollSeconds, cut to calibrate.maxSeconds, peak at calibrate.peakDb, classified by the B1 rule
#             tree (describe + extra + classify, the same 18 classes as zrmd/audio). Writes prepared/*.wav and
#             manifest.json (source URL, licence, licence file, sha256, class, why) in the refcache, never in the workshop.
#   targets : cuts the four OneKey event windows of V_017 (E001, E005, E006, E007) from the demucs stem each event was
#             detected on, for analysis only, into calibrate.targetsDir (refcache, never archived, never published).
#   fit     : picks a stratified subset (calibrate.perClass per class, filled to calibrate.maxSounds), fits every sound
#             with every single bank block, then the best single paired with the calibrate.pairsFromBest next blocks
#             (warm start). Fits are cached in fits.jsonl keyed by sound, chain and the sha256 of each block.py and of
#             the fit settings, so a rerun after A1 or A2 adds blocks only fits the new chains.
#             Writes calibration-report.json and prints the per-class table (score distribution, best chain, classes
#             under calibrate.passScore, which become the list of missing blocks).
# --check proves the report logic both ways: a synthetic bank-rendered target must pass and pure noise fitted by a
# sine-only bank must fail (exit 0 when both directions behave, 1 otherwise); check --sabotage must exit 1.
# Usage: python -I Scripts/CalibrateSoundBank.py prepare|targets|fit|report|check [--max N] [--blocks a,b] [--markdown]
#        fit --targets-only (OneKey windows only), report --lines A:B (rebuild from a fits.jsonl slice), --out name.json
# FR : calibration de la banque sur un corpus CC0 ; préparation, cibles OneKey, ajustement incrémental, tableau par classe.
